{"name":"Blue Hosta","hex":"#2a6f73","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"hosta","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several herbaceous Asiatic plants of the genus Hosta."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hosta"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hosta","blocks":[{"text":"Hosta is a genus of plants commonly known as hostas, plantain lilies and occasionally by the Japanese name gibōshi. Hostas are widely cultivated as shade-tolerant foliage plants. The genus is placed in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Agavoideae, and is native to northeast Asia. Like many \"lilioid monocots\", the genus was once classified in the Liliaceae. The genus was named by Austrian botanist Leopold Trattinnick in 1812, in honor of the Austrian botanist Nicholas Thomas Host. In 1817, the generic name Funkia was used by German botanist Kurt Sprengel in honor of Heinrich Christian Funck, a collector of ferns and alpines. It was later used as a common name and is referenced in some older literature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Hosta_Bressingham_Blue.JPG/330px-Hosta_Bressingham_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hosta","wikidata":"Q623347"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Afbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hosta sieboldiana by Abraham Jacobus Wendel, 1868","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAfbeelding-053-Hosta_sieboldiana.tif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg/330px-Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg/330px-Hosta_capitata2UME.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHosta_capitata2UME.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.w